      "title": "Part Time Spanish Instructor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\n\nLocation: Delaware\n\nOhio Wesleyan University is a selective, private, undergraduate liberal arts institution founded in 1842 and located just 20 miles north of Columbus. The Department of World Languages and Cultures at Ohio Wesleyan University is seeking one part-time Spanish Instructor to teach two sections of second-semester Spanish, with a strong likelihood of teaching one section of first-semester Spanish during the Spring 2027 semester. These courses are taught on campus and meet four times per week for 50 minutes each session on Mondays, Wednesdays, and Fridays. There may also be an opportunity to teach during the Fall 2027 semester. The ideal candidate will possess a master's degree in Spanish or Foreign Language Education, native or near-native fluency in Spanish, and prior college-level teaching experience. As a residential liberal arts institution, Ohio Wesleyan University delivers all instruction in a face-to-face, in-person environment. Online courses are not offered during the fall and spring semesters. Compensation is $5,000 per course. This position is not eligible for benefits. If you decide to join the Dept. of World Languages and Cultures , you will: Become part of a dynamic, supportive, and collaborative team of instructors who are committed to their students and to excellence in language teaching.  Join a liberal arts college that is deeply committed to creating an environment that acknowledges, encourages, and celebrates differences.  Teach small classes (up to 22 students per class). Be mentored by and receive support from the Chair and other members of the department as needed. Have office space for use while you are on campus. Receive a free parking pass that allows you to park anywhere on campus. To apply, please submit a cover letter expressing your interest in the position and a current curriculum vitae, including the names and contact information of three professional references, to Dr. Eva Paris  ( eeparish@owu.edu ),  Chair of the Department of World Languages and Cultures. The Spring 2027 semester begins on Wednesday, January 13, 2027. Applications will be reviewed as they are received and will be accepted until the position is filled. Ohio Wesleyan University offers a rewarding place to work. To learn more about why OWU could be your next professional home, please visit  our website .",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=a2082ac8-6363-4a1c-92d0-adacd9e4f844&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201858549405_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Public Safety Officer Part Time",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\n\nLocation: Delaware\n\nOhio Wesleyan University  ( www.owu.edu ) is a selective, private, undergraduate liberal arts, residential institution founded in 1842 and is located just 20 miles north of Columbus. The University’s Department of Public Safety invites applications for a Part-Time Public Safety Officer. This is a 20 hour a week position that works a varying schedule.  POSITION SUMMARY: As a non-sworn Public Safety Officer, you will be on a small team with big responsibilities - taking action when unsafe behaviors are observed and responding to emergency situations. We will rely on you to handle calls across the entire campus, deter crime, and build meaningful relationships. To thrive in this position, you will make our community feel safe, supported, and treated equitably. We pay $20/hr. This position benefits those who need or like non-traditional work schedules. Most shifts involve evening or night hours and may include weekends. Over time, employees with seniority may select shifts with more traditional hours. STARTING QUALIFICATIONS: Age Requirement: Must be at least 21 years of age at the time of hire Education :  High school diploma or GED required Experience:  Minimum 1 to 2 years of experience in customer service, social work, psychology, security, public safety, or working with young adults/student populations Interpersonal Skills:  Exceptional communication skills, empathy, conflict resolution, and active listening skills with a strong customer-service focus. Ability to work well in a team environment Physical Requirements:  Ability to stand and walk for extended periods, sit and operate a vehicle, climb stairs during building checks, and work outdoors in varying weather conditions Candidates must have an Ohio driver license  Candidates must pass a Background Check screening Hours:  Part-time (typically 10 to 29 hours per week; flexible, variable shifts including evenings, weekends, and nights)   Key Responsibilities Patrol & Parking Enforcement Enforce campus parking regulations, issue citations, and manage special event traffic routing. Conduct regular vehicle and foot patrols of academic buildings, residence halls, athletic facilities, and exterior grounds. Check and secure doors, windows, and facility entry points at designated intervals. Monitor campus property for safety hazards, maintenance issues, and suspicious activities or unauthorized persons. Student Engagement & Support Build positive, trusting relationships with students, faculty, and staff. Serve as a friendly, approachable campus resource and first point of contact for student needs or directions. Assist students with lockouts for residence halls and campus buildings upon verification of proper identification. Emergency Response & Policy Support Enforce and abide by all university policies, rules, and regulations with a de-escalation and education-first mindset. Respond promptly to campus calls, alarms, and routine requests for assistance. Contact and act as a professional liaison with local emergency services (police, fire, 911) when necessary. Administrative Duties Document all daily activities, shift logs, and security occurrences accurately. Write clear, objective incident, citation, and damage reports using computerized reporting software.   FACTORS CONTRIBUTING SUCCESS:  You use your wit and emotional intelligence to de-escalate situations You value diverse cultures and learning from others People feel heard and understood by you through active listening and non-verbal body language You evaluate and pick your methods of communication appropriate to situations - ranging from a voice of empathy to measured authority You don’t just say you have a passion for serving others - you act on it You are able to write clearly and concisely Completed application packets will include: Letter of interest (cover letter) Resume or Curriculum Vitae  The names, titles, and contact information for three professional references NOTE: Employment sponsorship is not available for this position. Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the United States. Please email application materials to: jobs@owu.edu Application review will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled.  WHAT WE OFFER: Located in the Columbus, Ohio metropolitan area, Ohio Wesleyan is a selective residential liberal arts college enrolling approximately 1,600+ students. The college’s signature program, The OWU Connection, empowers students to complete research, community service, careers, and study abroad, teaching students to apply their knowledge in real-world settings. Ohio Wesleyan is featured in the book Colleges That Change Lives and was recently listed as the No. 1 liberal arts college in Ohio for social mobility by U.S. News and World Report. OWU is located in Delaware, OH, a lively college town just 30 minutes from Columbus, the 14th most populous city in the United States and part of the fastest growing, most economically vibrant areas of the Midwest. Columbus, known increasingly as the “Silicon Midwest,” was named by LinkedIn as one of the top 10 U.S. cities in which to launch your career.   To achieve our mission, we continually strive to foster a campus community where every individual is respected, supported, and able to flourish. We affirm the worth of all persons and welcome people of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. Ohio Wesleyan University is an equal opportunity institution and seeks applications from individuals who will contribute to, and help strengthen, our commitment to academic excellence, mutual respect, and the shared pursuit of knowledge.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=a2082ac8-6363-4a1c-92d0-adacd9e4f844&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201856646448_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Data Science Research Assistant-SW",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\n\nLocation: Delaware\n\nPurpose/Objective of the Role:  The Data Lab is seeking two motivated students to assist with a real-world analytics project for a water utility company. This position places a strong emphasis on research, specifically identifying the right datasets and determining which factors most significantly influence model performance in the context of utility management and resource optimization. The successful candidates will work independently to extract insights and build predictive models that could have tangible industry impact.     Tasks & Responsibilities:   Data Discovery & Research:  Investigate potential data sources to determine the most suitable data for specific research problems related to water utility operations.  Data Exploration & Analysis:  Perform Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) to understand data structure.  Feature Selection: Analyze data to identify key variables and determine which factors influence the model's accuracy.  Model Building:  Design, implement, and validate predictive models using appropriate machine learning algorithms.  Documentation:  Clearly document all research methods, data sources, and results for future reference.  Qualifications/Skills: Education:  Currently enrolled as an undergraduate major in Data Analytics, Computer Science or Mathematics.  Coursework:  Successful completion of CS 300: Data Mining and Machine Learning (or equivalent coursework/experience).  Technical Skills:  Experience with Python-based data science libraries (e.g., NumPy, pandas, scikit-learn) is preferred.  Soft Skills:  Ability to work independently, manage time effectively, and document work clearly.  Potential Future Career Benefits:  Industry Application:  Apply theoretical knowledge to a live industry project with a water utility company, bridging the gap between classroom learning and real-world application.  End-to-End Data Science Skills:  Gain proficiency in the full data lifecycle, from data discovery and cleaning to feature selection and model building.  Portfolio Building:  Generate tangible results and research experience that can be discussed in future job interviews.  Mentorship:  Work directly with faculty to refine your technical and research methodologies.    NOTE: Employment sponsorship is not available for this position. Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the United States Application Instructions:  Complete the ADP application by selecting \"Apply\" button and answer all questions. Supervisor: Mehwish Abbasi Commitment: 5-8 hours per week Term: Fall 2026 only",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=a2082ac8-6363-4a1c-92d0-adacd9e4f844&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201853792179_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Accounting Operations Strategic Automation Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\n\nLocation: Delaware\n\nStrengthen financial operations. Modernize processes. Create measurable impact. DEPARTMENT Accounting REPORTS TO Controller APPOINTMENT Full-time | 12 months Help shape the future of financial operations at Ohio Wesleyan Ohio Wesleyan University is seeking an experienced, forward-thinking Accounting Operations & Strategic Automation Specialist to strengthen our financial operations while helping us modernize the way work gets done. This is an opportunity for an accounting or financial operations professional who combines strong technical knowledge with curiosity, initiative, and a passion for continuous improvement. You will provide leadership for essential accounting functions - including accounts payable, purchasing, reconciliations, and financial close activities - while identifying ways to use automation, data analytics, and AI-enabled tools to reduce manual work, improve accuracy, and support better decision-making. The successful candidate will be comfortable moving between day-to-day financial operations and broader strategic initiatives. You will collaborate with colleagues across the University, assess current processes, develop practical improvements, and help build a culture that embraces innovation, efficiency, and responsible financial stewardship. What You'll Do Strengthen accounting and financial operations Support accurate financial reconciliations and proactively identify and resolve variances. Establish, maintain, and continuously improve reconciliation procedures and timelines. Support timely monthly reporting and year-end close and audit activities. Oversee accounts payable and procurement processes, including invoice processing, vendor management, payment practices, and cash-flow considerations. Provide lead direction and guidance to assigned staff and student employees. Advance strategic sourcing and cost optimization Analyze University spending to identify cost-saving opportunities, purchasing efficiencies, duplicate vendors, and opportunities to consolidate services. Support requests for proposals for major purchasing categories, evaluate vendor responses, and recommend sourcing strategies aligned with University priorities. Monitor contract renewals, vendor performance, cost increases, and related budget impacts. Strengthen vendor relationships while improving payment terms, processes, and financial visibility. Lead automation and process modernization Identify, design, and implement automation opportunities across accounting and financial workflows. Develop automated accounts payable entry, approval, and payment processes, including ACH, wire, and check workflows with improved tracking. Use data analytics, exception reporting, intelligent matching, and standardized templates to improve reconciliation processes. Explore practical uses of AI-enabled accounting tools, robotic process automation, and other emerging technologies. Partner with Finance, Information Services, budget managers, and other campus colleagues to ensure that technology recommendations support institutional priorities and include appropriate training and resources. Help colleagues understand and adopt new processes by building engagement, providing guidance, and demonstrating the value of change. What You'll Bring Required qualifications Bachelor's degree in accounting, finance, business administration, or a related field. Seven to ten years of progressively responsible experience in accounting, financial operations, procurement, strategic sourcing, or a closely related area. Strong proficiency with ERP systems, financial software, electronic procurement platforms, and advanced Microsoft Excel functions, including pivot tables and lookup functions. Demonstrated success designing and implementing process improvements, automation, or system enhancements. Experience using - or the ability to effectively evaluate and apply - AI-enabled accounting tools and emerging automation technologies. Strong financial and analytical skills, including the ability to assess costs and benefits, evaluate contracts, monitor performance measures, and translate data into recommendations. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Ability to manage multiple complex projects and priorities with limited direction. A collaborative, customer-service-focused, and solution-oriented approach. Additional experience that will help you succeed Accounting, procurement, or financial operations experience in higher education or another complex organization. Experience with expense management systems such as Concur. Experience with accounts payable automation or electronic procurement platforms such as Jaggaer. Experience leading change initiatives, building organizational support, and encouraging innovation. Experience providing direction or guidance to staff or student employees. Why This Role Matters The work of this position will have an impact across the University. By strengthening core accounting practices, improving purchasing decisions, and thoughtfully applying technology, the Accounting Operations & Strategic Automation Specialist will help Ohio Wesleyan: Improve the accuracy and timeliness of financial information. Reduce repetitive and manual work. Strengthen vendor and purchasing practices. Identify meaningful cost-saving opportunities. Improve service to campus partners. Build more responsive, efficient, and sustainable financial operations. If you are energized by the opportunity to combine accounting expertise, strategic thinking, and emerging technology to create measurable organizational improvements, we encourage you to apply. To Apply Completed application packets must include: A letter of interest A cover letter A current resume The names, titles, and contact information for three professional references who may be contacted later in the selection process. References will not be contacted without the candidate's prior awareness. Candidates who experience difficulty uploading all required materials through our career page may submit supplemental materials to jobs@owu.edu . Application review will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Work authorization:  Employment sponsorship is not available for this position. Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the United States. What We Offer Ohio Wesleyan is a selective, residential liberal arts college enrolling approximately 1,600 students. The college's signature program, The OWU Connection , empowers students to complete research, community service, career experiences, and study abroad, teaching students to apply their knowledge in real-world settings. Ohio Wesleyan is featured in the book Colleges That Change Lives and was recently listed by U.S. News & World Report as the No. 1 liberal arts college in Ohio for social mobility. To discover why Ohio Wesleyan offers a rewarding place to work - and why it could be your next work home - visit our What Makes Working at OWU Great webpage. OWU is located in Delaware, Ohio, a lively college town just 30 minutes north of Columbus - the 14th most populous city in the United States and part of one of the fastest-growing, most economically vibrant regions in the Midwest. Columbus, increasingly known as the \"Silicon Midwest,\" was named by LinkedIn as one of the top 10 U.S. cities in which to launch a career. Our Commitment To achieve our mission, we continually strive to foster a campus community where every individual is respected, supported, and able to flourish. We affirm the worth of all persons and welcome people of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. Ohio Wesleyan University is an equal opportunity institution and seeks applications from individuals who will contribute to and help strengthen our commitment to academic excellence, mutual respect, and the shared pursuit of knowledge.",
      "department": ""
    },
